<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  UNTESTED until an elevated local install passes. Before the first `choco push`, test it on
  a real Windows account, ideally a second one rather than the account that built the
  package, elevating as that same account: the package currently supports same-account
  elevation only (see the KEY RISK note in tools/chocolateyInstall.ps1).
    1. Run `choco pack` in packaging/chocolatey/.
    2. Before the real install, run the step-3 install command as SYSTEM, from that same
       directory (for example through a SYSTEM scheduled task, or PsExec -s), and confirm it
       exits 1 before npm runs: it prints the "Refusing to install amicus as SYSTEM" message
       and no "Running: npm" line. Do this first: once amicus is installed, choco reports it
       as already installed and never runs the script.
    3. From an elevated prompt in that same directory (`choco install` needs an admin shell
       by default), run
       `choco install amicus -s "'.;https://community.chocolatey.org/api/v2/'"`.
       `-s` is choco's short form of its source option, used here because an XML comment
       cannot contain a double hyphen; the community feed after the semicolon is what lets
       the nodejs-lts dependency resolve.
    4. From the interactive account, in an ordinary (non-elevated) prompt, `where.exe amicus`
       must resolve before `amicus init` is run (`where.exe`, because in PowerShell `where` is
       an alias for Where-Object). Then confirm `amicus init` finds Node on PATH and
       completes.
    5. After the install, open an ordinary (non-elevated) prompt, run `amicus doctor`, and
       confirm it finds the OpenCode engine binary, because the package installs through
       `npm install -g`, npm 11 warns about install scripts that are not on its
       allow-scripts list (a later phase of that policy is to block them), and
       opencode-ai's install script is what places the engine binary.

  Fields sourced from:
    - id/authors/projectUrl: package.json (name, author, repository)
    - title: server.json "title"
    - tags: seven of package.json's "keywords", plus amicus, anthropic, mcp, cli and nodejs
    - summary: the first sentence of server.json's "description"
    - description: written for this package, adapting package.json's "description" (which
      .claude-plugin/plugin.json repeats) and README.md's opening paragraph
    - copyright: LICENSE (root of amicus repo)
    - dependency floor: package.json "engines".node (>=22.12.0)
  iconUrl deliberately omitted for v1 — Chocolatey moderation treats it as a guideline,
  not a requirement, and no existing amicus asset is a good square-icon candidate without
  the owner picking one. Add it in a later package revision.
-->
<package xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/packaging/2015/06/nuspec.xsd">
  <metadata>
    <id>amicus</id>
    <!-- Keep in lockstep with package.json/server.json/.claude-plugin/plugin.json at
         release time (docs/publishing.md's release checklist, steps 6 and 9) — no script
         syncs this today, same as the other three files it already lists as manual, but
         tests/scripts/package-manifest.test.js pins it to package.json. -->
    <version>4.14.1</version>
    <title>Amicus</title>
    <authors>Christian Wagner</authors>
    <owners>BourbonDog</owners>
    <projectUrl>https://github.com/BourbonDog/amicus</projectUrl>
    <projectSourceUrl>https://github.com/BourbonDog/amicus</projectSourceUrl>
    <docsUrl>https://github.com/BourbonDog/amicus#documentation</docsUrl>
    <bugTrackerUrl>https://github.com/BourbonDog/amicus/issues</bugTrackerUrl>
    <packageSourceUrl>https://github.com/BourbonDog/amicus/tree/main/packaging/chocolatey</packageSourceUrl>
    <licenseUrl>https://github.com/BourbonDog/amicus/blob/main/LICENSE</licenseUrl>
    <requireLicenseAcceptance>false</requireLicenseAcceptance>
    <copyright>Copyright (c) 2026 Christian Wagner; Copyright (c) 2025 John Renaldi</copyright>
    <tags>amicus claude claude-code anthropic llm multi-model council mcp cli nodejs sidecar opencode</tags>
    <summary>Multi-model LLM Council and parallel AI window for Claude Code.</summary>
    <description><![CDATA[
Amicus is a multi-model LLM Council and parallel AI window for Claude Code, installed as a
global npm CLI (`amicus` / `am`) plus an MCP server.

Hand Claude a plan, a diff, or a design and say "council review this": Amicus routes it
through several models from different families, has them anonymously cross-review each
other, and a non-Claude chair synthesizes a verdict. Or fork a single conversation to
Gemini, GPT, DeepSeek, or another model, work in parallel, and fold the result back.

This package installs the `amicus` npm package globally (`npm install -g amicus`) via its
Node.js LTS dependency (`nodejs-lts`). It currently supports same-account elevation only:
npm's global prefix is per-account (Node's bundled npmrc sets `prefix=${APPDATA}\npm`), so
under a different admin account or SYSTEM, amicus and its `amicus`/`am` shims land in that
account's `%APPDATA%\npm`, off the interactive user's PATH; the install refuses to run as
SYSTEM, which is never the interactive user. It does **not** run amicus's
postinstall registration during `choco install` — after installing, open an ordinary
(non-elevated) prompt, check that `where.exe amicus` finds it, and run `amicus init`,
`amicus setup`, and `amicus doctor`.

Requires at least one model API key (OpenRouter recommended) or a local OpenAI-compatible
server (Ollama / LM Studio / vLLM) — see `amicus setup`.

Project: https://github.com/BourbonDog/amicus
License: MIT
    ]]></description>
    <releaseNotes>https://github.com/BourbonDog/amicus/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md</releaseNotes>
    <dependencies>
      <!-- amicus requires Node >=22.12.0 (package.json "engines"). nodejs-lts already
           exists on community.chocolatey.org (confirmed 2026-09-28); using it instead of
           bundling a binary is the whole point of this package per B-REL-5. -->
      <dependency id="nodejs-lts" version="22.12.0" />
    </dependencies>
  </metadata>
  <files>
    <file src="tools\**" target="tools" />
  </files>
</package>
